{"kind":"turn_complete","ts":"2026-08-12T08:37:50.274960830Z","turn":1,"data":{"text":"Hello! I've been examining the `hanihi` crates and I also noticed you asked about my working directory. Let me answer both parts.\n\n## Working Directory\n\nThe tools I have access to operate within the context of this git repository, which is the **`hanihi`** project itself. The repository root contains the workspace `Cargo.toml`, a `README.md`, and directories for `crates/`, `evals/`, `plans/`, and `working/`. I can list and read files from this repository.\n\n## The `hanihi` Crates\n\nThe workspace is a Rust project with four members. Here's what I've found:\n\n### 1. `crates/hanihi-core` — *library crate*\nThe core library, providing:\n- **`agent.rs`** — the `Agent` loop (persistent history, tool dispatch, `max_turns` guard, streaming support via `tokio::spawn` + `mpsc`)\n- **`error.rs`** — error types\n- **`lib.rs`** — library entry point\n- **`mcp.rs`** — Model Context Protocol client functionality\n- **`session/`** — `SessionManager`, `lock.rs` (filesystem locking), `log.rs` (append-only JSONL event log), `mod.rs`\n- **`source.rs`** — source-tree access (git-repo-aware file reading/listing, respect `.gitignore` and `.ignore`)\n- **`tool.rs`** — tool definitions, including built-ins like `get_time`, `echo`, `read_file`, `list_dir`\n\n### 2. `crates/hanihi-cli` — *binary crate*\n- **`main.rs`** — the CLI entry point with clap argument parsing and a reedline REPL. Supports `--once` one-shot mode, session management, and MCP server attachment.\n\n### 3. `crates/hanihi-eval` — *binary crate*\n- **`main.rs`** — the eval runner that executes TOML-based test cases against a live LLM, asserting tool calls, text content, error-free completion, etc., against the session log.\n\n### 4. `crates/mcp-echo-server` — *binary crate*\n- **`main.rs`** — a minimal MCP stdio server demonstrating a single `mcp_echo` tool (not published to crates.io).\n\nThe project uses **rig** (0.41) for the completion loop, **rmcp** (3.1) for MCP, and **reedline** (0.49) for the REPL. Built-in tools include `get_time`, `echo`, `read_file`, and `list_dir`, with source-tree access respecting ignore rules via the `ignore` crate.\n\nWhat would you like to know more about? I can look into any specific crate or source file in more detail.","tool_calls":15}}
